This invention presents a technique for conveying both analog and digital data alongside energy transfer from a power source (10) to a power receiver (12) that maintains galvanic isolation from said source. The technique encompasses these procedural steps:

a) Obtaining the analog signal (22a) and digital signal (24a) intended for transmission;

b) Establishing the total count nAD of potential discrete values ADi (where i ranges from 1 to nAD) achievable through analog-to-digital conversion, along with specifying the range these ADi values may span;

c) Allocating a binary-format digital value Dx corresponding to the digital data;

d) Establishing the lowest permissible count of events, nmin, that must be conveyed;

e) Calculating the highest permissible count of events, nmax, that will be conveyed;

f) Creating a first collection of values nlow according to the formula: nlow(ADi) = nmin + f1(ADi), for i = 1 through nAD;

g) Creating a second collection of values nhigh according to the formula: nhigh(ADi) = nmax − f2(ADi), for i = 1 through nAD;

h) Converting the analog signal into digital form to produce a digitized value ADx;

i) Calculating the event count as nlow(ADx) = nmin + f1(ADx) when the digital bit Dx currently being sent equals 0; alternatively, calculating the event count as nhigh(ADx) = nmax − f2(ADx) when the digital bit Dx currently being sent equals 1 (or applying the reverse logic); and

j) Broadcasting a signal that corresponds directly to the event count established in step i).

Additionally, this invention encompasses a corresponding apparatus designed to execute this method.

